 (PATHOLOGY RECORDS, RADIOLOGY RECORDS, GENETICS RECORDS), to broader test-type categories (PATHOLOGY, RADIOLOGY, GENETICS), and to workflow guidance (ORGANIZE BY DATE) and PROCEDURES as a binder component. The high combined degree on these edges indicates that FOLDERS are the unifying scaffold connecting all other entities in the community. [Data: Relationships (617, 618, 619, 2458, 2462,+more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The system uses test-type categorization to reduce ambiguity and improve retrieval\",\"explanation\":\"A key design pattern in the community is test-type grouping: FOLDERS are recommended for separating documentation by test types such as pathology, radiology, and genetics, with each being treated as a distinct division within the binder. This creates a taxonomy where clinical information is partitioned along diagnostic modality lines rather than being intermingled. [Data: Entities (216, 1517, 1518, 1519)]\\n\\nThis structure is operationalized through explicit links between FOLDERS and the three test-type categories PATHOLOGY, RADIOLOGY, and GENETICS, which are described as suggested folder/test categories for organizing records. This indicates the binder system is intended to be consistent (standard categories) and scalable (categories can be applied across multiple records over time). [Data: Relationships (2458, 2459, 2460)]\"},{\"summary\":\"PATHOLOGY RECORDS are a dedicated folder category for biopsy/tissue analysis documentation\",\"explanation\":\"PATHOLOGY RECORDS are defined as a medical binder folder category intended to organize documentation related to pathology testing, including biopsy and tissue analysis, and to encompass pathology reports and pathology review materials documenting examination, interpretation, and reporting of tissue/biopsy specimens. This definition indicates the category is focused on capturing definitive diagnostic documentation derived from specimen analysis. [Data: Entities (459)]\\n\\nThe relationship between FOLDERS and PATHOLOGY RECORDS specifies that pathology is a test type typically arranged within folders and that the binder system groups such documents under a pathology folder category. This ties the conceptual category (pathology) to a concrete filing practice (pathology folder holding pathology records). [Data: Relationships (617)]\"},{\"summary\":\"RADIOLOGY RECORDS consolidate imaging and scan-related reports into a single organized set\",\"explanation\":\"RADIOLOGY RECORDS are defined as imaging-related medical documents that are recommended to be organized together as a dedicated folder category, including radiology and other scan reports and related imaging documentation. This implies the folder is meant to centralize interpretation outputs and supporting imaging paperwork to prevent fragmentation across unrelated sections. [Data: Entities (460)]\\n\\nThe explicit FOLDERS → RADIOLOGY RECORDS relationship confirms radiology is a test type typically organized within folders and that radiology records are grouped into a dedicated radiology folder category for test results and documentation. This provides a clear rule for where imaging outputs should live in the binder hierarchy. [Data: Relationships (618)]\"},{\"summary\":\"GENETICS RECORDS integrate test results with counseling materials, supporting longitudinal reference\",\"explanation\":\"GENETICS RECORDS are defined as a binder category for genetics-related documentation, including genetic testing records and associated genetic counseling materials (test orders, reports, results). This breadth suggests the binder system anticipates genetics workflows may involve both laboratory outputs and interpretive/support documentation that should remain linked. [Data: Entities (461)]\\n\\nThe FOLDERS → GENETICS RECORDS relationship states that genetics records are categorized under the genetics test type and typically filed within folders, aligning with the binder-style approach that consolidates by test type into corresponding folder categories. This establishes genetics as a first-class category within the overall filing system. [Data: Relationships (619)]\"},{\"summary\":\"PROCEDURES are tracked as a distinct category and are explicitly tied to date-based ordering\",\"explanation\":\"PROCEDURES are defined as the patient’s clinical and medical procedures—such as biopsies and other interventions—that the patient is advised to document, track, and organize in a medical binder, filed in designated folders and arranged by date to maintain a chronological record. This makes procedures not just a topic area but a time-sequenced log of interventions. [Data: Entities (462)]\\n\\nThe relationship between FOLDERS and PROCEDURES explicitly notes that procedures are recommended as a category to organize within the binder, with the system instructing users to create folders dedicated to procedures and organize the contents by date. This indicates the binder model supports both category-based separation and within-category chronology, particularly for interventions. [Data: Relationships (2461)]\"},{\"summary\":\"“Organize by date” is a cross-cutting governance rule for how content is stored inside folders\",\"explanation\":\"ORGANIZE BY DATE is defined as a record-keeping practice: placing items in folders in date order to track the care timeline. This functions as a governance layer that standardizes ordering within each folder, enabling a user to reconstruct clinical progression. [Data: Entities (1535)]\\n\\nThe FOLDERS → ORGANIZE BY DATE relationship specifies that the text recommends organizing items within binder folders by date. Combined with the description of folders and procedures, this suggests chronological sequencing is a general binder rule, not limited to a single record type. [Data: Relationships (2462); Entities (216, 462)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Community impact is operational and informational rather than adversarial or compliance-related\",\"explanation\":\"All entities and relation